{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"clustering","title":"K-Means Clustering","group":"Machine Learning","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">K-Means groups unlabelled points into K clusters around centres (centroids). The elbow method helps choose K.</p>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Start</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Pick K initial centroids (<code>k-means++</code> spreads them out smartly).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Assign</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Each point joins its nearest centroid.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Update</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Each centroid moves to the mean of its points.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Repeat</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Until assignments stop changing (or <code>max_iter</code>).</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import Counter\nfrom sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n\nX, _ = make_blobs(n_samples=300, centers=4, cluster_std=0.60, random_state=0)\nkm = KMeans(n_clusters=4, init=\"k-means++\", n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(X)\nprint(km.cluster_centers_.shape)          # (4, 2)\nprint(sorted(Counter(km.labels_).values()))   # [75, 75, 75, 75]   ← 4 equal clusters</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-line\"></i>Choosing K: the elbow method</h3>\n<p>Run K-Means for K = 1…10 and record the <strong>WCSS</strong> (within-cluster sum of squares, <code>inertia_</code>). It always falls as K grows; the “elbow” where it stops falling sharply is a good K.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n\nX, _ = make_blobs(n_samples=300, centers=4, cluster_std=0.60, random_state=0)\nwcss = [KMeans(n_clusters=k, n_init=10, random_state=0).fit(X).inertia_ for k in range(1, 11)]\ndrops = [round(wcss[i] - wcss[i + 1]) for i in range(5)]\nprint(drops)       # [1621, 644, 335, 23, 18]   ← big drops until K = 4, then small ones</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nplt.plot(range(1, 11), wcss, marker=\"o\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Number of clusters (K)\"); plt.ylabel(\"WCSS\"); plt.title(\"Elbow Method\")\nplt.show()</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math\"></i>The same with SciPy</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.cluster.vq import kmeans, vq\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_blobs\n\nX, _ = make_blobs(n_samples=300, centers=4, cluster_std=0.60, random_state=0)\ncentroids, _ = kmeans(X, 4, seed=0)       # centroids\nlabels, _ = vq(X, centroids)              # nearest centroid for each point\nprint(centroids.shape, len(np.unique(labels)))   # (4, 2) 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>K-Means assumes round, similar-sized clusters and is sensitive to feature scales — standardise features first (<code>StandardScaler</code>). For odd shapes, try DBSCAN.</p></div></section>"}